WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:10.889
Today is a glorious day, because we are going
to witness the first table cloth pull simulation

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:12.790
I have ever seen.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.360
Well, I hoped that it would go a little more
glorious than this.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.820
Maybe if we pull a bit quicker.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.570
Yes, there we go, and…we’re good.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.570
Loving it.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.740
Now, if you are a seasoned Fellow Scholar,
you might remember from about 50 videos ago

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.940
that we covered the predecessor of this paper,
called Incremental Potential Contact, IPC

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.440
in short.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.920
So, what could it do?

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.640
It could perform seriously impressive squishing
experiments.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.960
And it also passed the tendril test, where
we threw a squishy ball at a glass wall, and

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.649
watched this process from the other side.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:57.770
A beautiful, and rare sight indeed.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.309
Unless you have cats and glass tables at home,
of course.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:03.309
Outstanding.

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:09.910
So, I hear you asking, Károly, are you trying
to say that this new paper tops all that?

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.620
Yes, that is exactly what I am saying.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.560
The table cloth pulling is one thing, but
it can do so much more.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:22.250
You can immediately start holding on to your
papers, and let’s go.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:30.090
This new variant of IPC is capable of simulating
super thin materials, and all this in a penetration-free

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.090
manner.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.310
Now, why is that so interesting or difficult?

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:39.900
Well, that is quite a challenge, remember
this earlier paper with the barbarian ship.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.210
Tons of penetration artifacts.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.150
And that is not even a thin object.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.800
Not nearly as thin as this stack would be.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:53.080
Let’s see what a previous simulation method
would do with this if these are 10 millimeters

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.080
each.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.230
That looks reasonable, now let’s cut the
thickness of the sheets in half.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:05.180
Yes, some bumpy artifacts appear, and at 1
millimeter, my goodness.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.160
It’s only getting worse.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.501
And, when we plug in the same thin sheets
into the new simulator.

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:17.340
All of them look good, and what’s more,
they can be simulated together with other

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.590
elastic objects without any issues.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.110
And this was a low-stress simulation.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.739
If we use the previous technique for a higher-stress
simulation.

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:30.799
This starts out well, until…uh-oh.

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:35.999
The thickness of the cloth is seriously decreasing
over time.

00:02:35.999 --> 00:02:37.469
That is not realistic.

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:42.870
But, if we plug the same scene into the new
technique, now that is realistic.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.959
So, what is all this good for?

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:51.790
Well, if we wish to simulate a bowl of noodles,
tons of thick objects, let’s see if we can

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.790
hope for an intersection-free simulation.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.879
Let’s look under the hood…and, there we
go!

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:00.760
All of the noodles are separated.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:07.489
But wait a second, I promised you thin objects,
these are not thin.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:10.060
Yes, now these, are thin.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.489
Still, no intersections.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:15.120
That is absolutely incredible.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.439
Other practical applications include simulating
hair.

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:20.359
Braids in particular.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:23.909
Granular materials against a thin sheet work
too.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:29.569
And, if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because the

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:35.909
authors promise that we can even simulate
this in-hand shuffling technique in a virtual

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:36.909
world.

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:39.689
Well, I will believe it when I see it.

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:42.510
Let’s see…my goodness.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.069
Look at that.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:49.519
Love the attention to detail where the authors
color coded the left and right stack so we

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.079
can better see how they mix, and if they intersect.

00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:56.290
Spoiler alert: they don’t.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.640
What a time to be alive!

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.810
It can also simulate this piece of cloth with
a ton of detail, and not only that, with large

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:09.549
timesteps, which means that we can advance
the time after each simulation step in bigger

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:13.700
packets, thereby speeding up the execution
time of the method.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:19.579
I also love how we get a better view of the
geometry changes as the other side of the

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.450
cloth has a different color.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.650
Once again, great attention to detail.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:31.390
We are still in the minutes per frame region,
and note that this runs on your processor,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:36.840
therefore, if someone can implement this on
the graphics card in a smart way, it could

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.879
become close to real time in at most a couple
papers more down the line.

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:48.340
And, this is a research paper that the authors
give away to all of us, free of charge.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.560
How cool is that.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.560
Thank you.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.139
Thank you so much for creating these miracles
and just giving them away for free.

00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:45.250
What a noble endeavor research is!

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.310
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

